WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.749 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i grew up being a self-centered kid in  school  sure i could do fast math  but i did not give a [ __ ] about others  who could not  i could calculate the cube root of 738  in my head when i was just 12.  but when my friends  struggling with basic fractions and  decimals asked me for help i turned a  blind eye  i hadn't mastered calculus yet  the national math olympiad was around  the corner  and my parents and teachers had big  expectations of me  i needed to focus on myself  and my friends would have done the same  thing if they were in my place  i received straight a's in the math and  sciences  but if there had been a class for  helping people  i would have got an f  the national education system in india  like in many parts of the world is  predicated on the idea of relative  performance and competition  and this dominates our view of success  i didn't think it was possible to break  out of this belief  until i met two people four years back  anju and zabi  two kids nearly half my age  anju just 15 years old lived in a  village that did not send girls to  school  but rather than try to move to a  different village and get her education  she devoted her energy in changing the  minds of people in her community  her determination changed the system  and she has now had more than 200 girls  to enroll in school and become educated  zabi just 16  convinced his school principal to start  an animal shelter in the school campus  where he and his fellow classmates have  rescued more than 300 injured dogs to  date  i was  fascinated with anju and zabi  for the first time in my life i met  teenagers who cared deeply about others  and took action to make things better  i certainly wasn't like them when i was  in school  and neither were the kids i knew  so why was that  why were anju and zabi the outliers and  not the mainstream  the more i thought about it it all  pointed to our education systems and the  extreme competitive pressure that is put  on kids  you got good grades  great now get better grades  you made it into a good college fine now  get into a better college  life  becomes this competitive race  and this race and girls children in an  inward looking me focus mindset  rather than developing an outward  looking we focus mindset  children leave schools with impressive  grades and certificates and get decent  jobs  but they miss becoming empathetic  responsible conscious citizens of this  world  but should school systems choose  between academic accidents  and empathy and compassion  i didn't think so and i chose to do  something about it  i quit my job and started a social  enterprise to try and tackle this issue  as part of our research i read a lot  about people who have dedicated their  lives towards the betterment of society  and how they developed their passion for  it  that's when i was introduced to bill  drayton  bill is credited by many for coining the  term social entrepreneur back in the  1970s  ashoka the non-profit he founded  supports social entrepreneurs in more  than 40 countries today  but what's interesting is this when bill  was 11  he had started an initiative to help his  classmates by creating a class newspaper  he skipped a lot of classes because of  his strong commitment and wasn't even  home most evenings  when bill's mother died he found  correspondence between her and her  school principal his mother was  concerned she asked why is a fifth  grader not in your school or our home  the school principal wrote back saying  trust your son  and please don't show your anxious  bill was unaware of this at the time but  when he thinks back on it he says  bless that principle who encouraged my  ability to make an impact in this world  we found two patterns in the stories of  people like bill  number one  most developed their passion for  creating change by going through an  outward-centric change-making experience  at a very young age  and number two they had at least one  adult who believed and supported them in  their journey  inspired by these earnings our team  designed a program for middle school and  high school children  our idea was to encourage the kids to  identify social or environmental issues  in their home school or community  support them as it took action to try  and tackle these issues  and appreciate them for their efforts  our role as facilitators was to do three  simple things encourage support and  appreciate  or what we called the esa framework in  short  who could be against such a program  right  yet we couldn't convince a single school  in the first two months  schools rejected us outright saying that  they did not see any value and that  children needed to focus solely on their  academics  we finally offered the entire program to  schools for free for an entire year to  get them to try it out  not an ideal start for us but we took  what we could get  if convincing schools and parents was  tricky  thankfully working with children was the  exact opposite  children loved our program  they quickly identified the problems  they wanted to tackle and passionately  got to work  chandan just 11 years old told us  i want everyone in my school to be  physically fit  his school did not offer a physical  fitness program so he gathered a group  of other sixth graders and started a  student-run yoga class for everyone  even two years since our program ended  the children still run their yoga class  and in fact have expanded their  initiative to five nearby schools  incredible  chandan's teacher recently told us that  chandan was an extremely shy kid at  first  but once he got a platform to solve a  problem he cared about  he discovered a new side of himself  chandan is no longer that timid child  he's an empowered agent of change  kirti just 15 started the code red  initiative to spread awareness about  menstrual hygiene in underprivileged  communities near her school  she was so successful that now three  years since her program she is helping  an ngo create awareness about celebrity  policy  dvd vicente told me  in my 10 years of schooling i never had  an adult who encouraged me to think  about helping my community but as soon  as i received that encouragement  my life changed and now i want to  continue tackling meaningful issues for  the rest of my life  this was common amongst all the students  after working with more than 25 000  children over three years  our biggest realization was this  all children are born with empathy and  compassion  the challenge is to remain with it as we  grow up  these humane values can either thrive  and flourish with encouragement and  support from adults  or  they can be completely stifled and  suppressed  which is what happens through our  education systems  which one do we all want for our  children  for our community  we have big problems today  political polarization  growing in inequality  climate change  we must fundamentally change the way we  are raising our children  we must set them free from this inward  looking me focused mindset to an outward  looking refocus mindset  looking back i don't believe my career  would have been any less successful if i  had more empathy and compassion as a  child  but i can't help but wonder how much  better the lives of my other young  friends would have been  would their careers be better  would they have passed along the empathy  they had experienced onto their own  children  academicians researchers and advocates  are fighting to include empathy and  compassion into the formal curriculum at  the institutional level  and each one of us you and me can make a  difference at the individual level  implement esa  encourage the children in your life to  think about and help others  support them when they start something  meaningful  and appreciate them for when they put in  the efforts  be their champion the one who keeps  their empathy alive so that no matter  who they become professionally or where  they go in the future  they create a better world for everyone  thank you very much  [Music]  you

